      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-182632",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{n} = \\begin{bmatrix}2^{n} & 2^{n} - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}for alln \u2208 N$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n = 1$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{1}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n =$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{k}$ {{field-4}} {{field-5}}\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2^{k + 1} & 2^{k + 1} - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ {{field-6}} {{field-7}}\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{n} = \\begin{bmatrix}2^{n} & 2^{n} - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nfor all $n \u2208 N$."
